WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.460
Screenwriters expend a lot of time and effort
to craft perfect scripts.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.220
However, some of the silver screen's most
memorable lines were actually ad-libbed by

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:08.860
the actors.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.040
There were many to choose from, but we've
gathered a dozen of the best improvised moments

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.980
in movie history to share with you.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.160
One of the delights of making a movie with
experienced comedians trained in improv is

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.670
that you're going to end up with a whole lot
of surprises.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.240
And because everyone is accustomed to thinking
on their toes, these bits of improvisation

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.060
can end up going pretty far out there.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:33.000
Bridesmaids opened the door for a new breed
of raunchy female-oriented romantic comedies

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.480
that broke the mold for the genre, and some
of its most memorable scenes were improvised.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.940
There was so much ad-libbing that director
Paul Feig prepared for it by using two cameras

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.140
for many of the one-on-one scenes, so he could
capture and cut together both reactions.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:50.540
Many of Melissa McCarthy's over-the-top moments
during the dress fitting scene, as well as

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.020
her banter with the air marshal on the plane,
were all unscripted.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.640
But the improv winner in Bridesmaids is the
horrible and hilarious scene where bride-to-be

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.070
Lillian, played by Maya Rudolph, makes an
unexpected stop in the middle of the street

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:04.670
after getting food poisoning.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:10.140
"It's happening, it's happening…It happened."

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.460
"What are you doing?"

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.260
"It happened."

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.160
This unscripted and perfectly timed moment
caught the cinematographer by surprise as

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.440
much as it did the audience.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.360
Matthew McConaughey has built an impressive
filmography over the years, but no matter

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:28.360
how many awards he wins, he'll never escape
the simple words he uttered during 1993's

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.360
Dazed and Confused.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.880
"All right, all right, all right."

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:36.180
It's become one of the movie's most quoted
lines, and McConaughey improvised it on the

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:36.680
spot.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:41.100
The actor claims to have based the line on
an utterance by Doors frontman Jim Morrison

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.930
during a live performance of the song "Roadhouse
Blues."

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:48.500
As repeated by McConaughey's character, career
stoner David Wooderson, the line became a

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.710
catchphrase on set.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.980
In fact, it showed up twice in the film.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.740
"All right, all right, all right."

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:59.260
The cast and crew even started using it off-camera,
and it's since become one of McConaughey's

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:04.280
signature lines in real life, he even revisited
it in his Academy Award speech for Best Actor.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:10.280
Viggo Mortenson is well known for his method
acting skills in films like Eastern Promises

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.280
and The Road, during which he insisted on
doing his own stunt work and fight scenes.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.130
While he didn't go full method while filming
The Lord of the Rings trilogy, he would often

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.860
ride his horse and hike alone to the next
filming location.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.360
However, there was one scene in The Two Towers
where Mortenson channeled actual physical

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.040
pain into his performance.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.060
In the first part of the movie, Pip and Merry
are held captive by a band of orcs and Aragorn

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:33.720
is desperately trying to save them.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.400
When he thinks they've been killed, he takes
his anger out on a nearby helmet.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.140
"ARGHHHH!"

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.770
The unscripted moment of Aragorn yelling and
falling to his knees wasn't method acting,

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.100
it was serious pain.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:52.040
This was the fifth take of the scene, and
Mortensen broke two toes when he kicked the

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:52.540
helmet.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:57.200
Director Peter Jackson decided to keep the
scene in the movie for its raw emotion, since

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.580
Mortenson didn't break character and his reaction
made complete sense for that moment.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.000
Avengers: Infinity War broke box office records
around the world, and it also broke viewers'

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.920
hearts with the results of Thanos' infamous
finger snap.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:13.900
Because this was such a big-budget film with
so many moving parts at any given time, it's

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.780
surprising that there would have been room
for any improvisation at all, let alone in

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:20.370
some incredibly poignant and important scenes.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:24.360
For example, most of the snarky exchange between
Star-Lord and Thor was ad-libbed.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:28.800
The scene where Captain America points out
how Thor copied his beard was also improvised

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:29.660
by the two actors.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:33.940
Even Dave Bautista, as Drax, had an off-the-cuff
response to Iron Man…

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.200
"Yeah, I'll do you one better.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:36.400
Who's Gamora?"

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:38.020
"I'll do you one better.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:39.660
Why is Gamora?"

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:43.400
However, one crushing moment of improv particularly
stands out.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:47.660
In the Russo brothers' script, Spider-Man's
final words were just supposed to be "I don't

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.500
feel so good" and "I'm sorry."

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.780
But actor Tom Holland added some heartrending
lines to the scene…

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:54.840
"I don't want to go.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:55.650
I don't want to go.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:56.640
Sir, please.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.120
I don't want to go.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:03:59.320
I don't want to go."

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:04.020
The rawness and vulnerability of the moment
are evident in Holland's performance and in

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.620
Robert Downey's emotional reaction to this
perfect unplanned bit of dialogue.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.960
Initially, some people questioned director
Christopher Nolan's decision to cast Heath

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.180
Ledger as the Joker in The Dark Knight.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:15.700
"Hiii."

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:20.150
But Ledger's incredible commitment to the
role was evident from the first moment he

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:21.120
appeared on screen.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.999
Nolan has credited Ledger with coming up with
most of what made this version of the Joker

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.340
so compelling, and he is widely rumored to
have improvised a couple of the movie's classic

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:29.840
moments.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.380
After the Joker is arrested by Detective Jim
Gordon, Gotham's mayor stops by the jail to

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:38.430
take a look at the city's new high-profile
prisoner, and promote Gordon to Commissioner.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:42.840
As the officers in the room break into applause,
they're joined by an allegedly improvising

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:43.340
Ledger.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:47.800
Elsewhere in the film, after he's rigged a
hospital to explode, the Joker hits the detonation

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.200
button, only to be disappointed when nothing
happens.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.240
According to rumor, Ledger's response was
another great moment of improvisation.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.800
Roy Scheider had many unforgettable scenes
in Jaws, from frantically trying to get beachgoers

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.820
out of shark-infested waters to taking aim
and blowing up his toothy nemesis.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:17.199
But, perhaps, the most memorable scene of
Jaws is when Scheider is chumming the water

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.880
and the enormous great white shark pops up
to say hello.

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:23.420
Scheider staggers backwards in shock and utters
this classic line…

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.200
"You're going to need a bigger boat."

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:30.619
But what most people don't know is that Scheider
actually ad-libbed that line numerous times

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.160
during different scenes in the film.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.780
After repeated production delays and cost
overruns threatened to sink the entire film,

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:41.009
the line "you're going to need a bigger boat"
became an in-joke among the cast and crew,

00:05:41.009 --> 00:05:44.300
and Scheider began randomly slipping it into
different scenes as a gag.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:48.560
It was so effective here, though, that director
Steven Spielberg decided to keep it.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:52.600
It's hard to believe, but one of the most
famous movie lines of all time almost ended

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:53.850
up on the cutting room floor.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:58.889
In the 1980 film adaptation of Stephen King's
novel The Shining, Jack Nicholson had a scene

00:05:58.889 --> 00:06:02.289
where his character was hacking through a
bathroom door with an axe.

00:06:02.289 --> 00:06:05.869
When he made an opening big enough for his
face, Nicholson ad-libbed a line that would

00:06:05.869 --> 00:06:08.960
have been known to every viewer of American
late-night television.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.060
"Heeere's Johnny."

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:15.660
That line was part of the classic opening
of The Tonight Show starring Johnny Carson

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:16.560
for decades.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:20.580
Unfortunately, one person who didn't get the
reference was director Stanley Kubrick.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.840
He had been living in England so long he had
no idea what Nicholson was talking about,

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.000
so it just seemed like a strange non sequitur.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.560
Fortunately, Nicholson's ad-lib made the final
cut and became one of the most quoted lines

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.500
out of a very quotable career.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:35.500
"You can't handle the truth."

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:37.980
"I want you to hold it between your knees."

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:40.720
"Wait 'til they get a load of me."

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:45.720
The Harry Potter movie universe is filled
with magical moments and an entire lexicon

00:06:45.729 --> 00:06:47.199
of spells based on J.K.

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:49.249
Rowling's best-selling series of books.

00:06:49.249 --> 00:06:53.319
The second installment in the movie series,
The Chamber of Secrets, follows the intrepid

00:06:53.319 --> 00:06:57.220
group of young wizards and witches as they
attempt to discover who unleashed a monster

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.369
that's terrorizing the students of Hogwarts.

00:06:59.369 --> 00:07:02.940
They suspect it has something to do with House
Slytherin and its ancestors.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:07.520
Harry and Ron have Hermione fix up a batch
of polyjuice potion so they can go undercover

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:08.530
and investigate.

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:12.920
The potion turns Harry and Ron into Draco
Malfoy's henchmen, Goyle and Crabbe, which

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:17.159
means they can find out if Malfoy's family
might have something to do with the beast.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:21.120
When Draco happens to see them, he notices
right away that Goyle is suddenly wearing

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:21.620
glasses.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:25.600
Goyle, Harry in disguise, says they're for
reading, to which Draco responds…

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:26.260
"Reading?"

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:26.980
"Mm-hm."

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:29.600
"I didn't know you could read."

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:33.880
Actor Tom Felton had forgotten his line, and
improvised a new one on the spot.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:37.840
Director Chris Columbus loved the cruelty
and comedy it brought to the scene, so he

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:38.540
left it in.

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:43.219
Harrison Ford has taken part in plenty of
classic movie moments but a couple of his

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:45.420
most iconic ones were actually improvised.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:50.099
In The Empire Strikes Back, there's a scene
that cemented Han Solo in our hearts forever

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:51.640
as the ultimate romantic rogue.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:55.939
Faced with what seems like his imminent death,
Carrie Fisher's Princess Leia finally admits

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:56.920
her love for Han.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:57.800
His response?

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:58.520
"I know."

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:00.320
But that wasn't in the script.

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:03.699
Han was actually supposed to say, "Just remember
that, 'cause I'll be back."

00:08:03.699 --> 00:08:07.719
Luckily for fans across the universe, Ford
thought he could do better and delivered the

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.140
line we all know and love today.

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:13.270
Then there's his perfectly wordless improvisation
in Raiders of the Lost Ark.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:17.539
Despite rehearsing the choreography for weeks,
Ford did not want to film a lengthy sword

00:08:17.539 --> 00:08:18.949
versus whip fight scene.

00:08:18.949 --> 00:08:23.110
He came down with food poisoning the night
before shooting and simply couldn't go through

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:23.800
with what they'd planned.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:28.120
He ran an idea past director Steven Spielberg
that became one of the most famous scenes

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:29.580
in the Indiana Jones series.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.300
In When Harry Met Sally, Billy Crystal's character
is driving from Chicago to New York with his

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:43.700
girlfriend's roommate, played by Meg Ryan.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:47.600
They definitely don't hit it off at first,
but as they keep running into each other through

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:49.800
the years, a friendship begins to develop.

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:54.680
Directed by Rob Reiner and written by Nora
Ephron, the film is filled with one-liners

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:59.800
about romance, love, and relationships addressing
the eternal question, "Can men and women really

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:00.380
be friends?"

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:04.900
Considering Crystal and Reiner's experience
with improv and stand-up comedy, it's no surprise

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:07.330
that a number of lines would be ad-libbed
along the way.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:11.600
In rehearsals, it was actually Crystal who
came up with the film's most iconic line…

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:13.800
"I'll have what she's having."

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:15.520
But that's not all.

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:19.840
The entire "pecan pie" discussion when Harry
and Sally are walking around the museum was

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:20.340
improvised.

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:25.000
At one point, when Crystal asks Ryan to repeat
his words, she briefly breaks character and

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.620
looks off camera at the director, then snaps
right back into the scene.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.120
"But I would be proud to partake of your pecan
pie."

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:33.520
"Oh, no."

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:34.920
"But I would be proud."

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:35.960
"But I would be proud."

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:40.300
It was such a genuine moment that Reiner decided
to use the whole take in the final film.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:45.300
Based on the timeless Roald Dahl book Charlie
and the Chocolate Factory, the similarly titled

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:50.311
Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory is a
whimsical and beloved film from 1971 that

00:09:50.311 --> 00:09:54.410
has consistently enchanted and even disturbed
audience members for decades.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:58.740
As to be expected from a comedy legend like
Gene Wilder, he had some things to say about

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:01.210
his character, the enigmatic Willy Wonka.

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:05.560
In particular, he wanted to introduce the
Wonka's unpredictable nature right away, before

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:06.820
he even spoke a word.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.820
Wilder proposed a bait and switch that wasn't
in the script.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:13.760
He would walk with a limp, as if he needed
a cane, then spring into action.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:26.060
It's a memorable sequence that also helps
illustrate Willy Wonka's complex personality.

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:27.070
As Wilder put it…

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:30.460
"From that time on, no one will know if I'm
lying or telling the truth."

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.880
It doesn't seem appropriate to say that This
Is Spinal Tap has a great ad-libbed line or

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:37.020
two because most of the film was improvised.

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:41.880
Directed by Rob Reiner and starring Michael
McKean, Christopher Guest and Harry Shearer

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:46.740
as members of the band Spinal Tap, the brilliant
mockumentary has a huge fan following and

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.960
spawned some truly classic catchphrases.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:50.860
"These go to eleven."

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:54.760
While there was an outline for the movie,
it was just a starting point for the cast

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:56.360
to follow their comedic instincts.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:11:00.700
Whether they were hanging out backstage, performing
live on stage, or even just looking for the

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:05.530
stage, the actors of Spinal Tap delivered
a masterclass on improvisation and continually

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:08.110
reminded viewers of one very important thing.

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:11.470
"There's such a fine line between stupid and
clever."

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:13.040
"Yeah, it's just that little turn about."

